When running `backstage-cli new` on Windows to generate a template that contains files inside nested subdirectories with template variables in their filenames (e.g. `scaffolder-field-extension-module` which has `src/extensions/{{extensionName}}.tsx.hbs`), the generated files on Windows are created with corrupted filenames and unparsed Handlebars variables directly inside `src/`:

```text
src/
├── extensions{{extensionName}}.test.tsx
├── extensions{{extensionName}}.tsx
├── index.ts
├── module.tsx
└── setupTests.ts
```

Instead of:

```text
src/
├── extensions/
│   ├── TeamInfoPickerFieldExtension.test.tsx
│   └── TeamInfoPickerFieldExtension.tsx
├── index.ts
├── module.tsx
└── setupTests.ts
```

### 👍 Expected Behavior

The CLI should create `src/extensions/` subdirectory containing `TeamInfoPickerFieldExtension.tsx` and `TeamInfoPickerFieldExtension.test.tsx`.

### 🥾 Reproduction steps

1. On a Windows machine running Backstage (v1.53.0/v1.53.1), execute:
   ```bash
   yarn backstage-cli new
   ```
2. Select `scaffolder-field-extension-module - A custom field extension for the Backstage Scaffolder`.
3. Enter module ID e.g. `team-info`.
4. Inspect the generated `src/` directory.
5. Notice the corrupted files `extensions{{extensionName}}.tsx` and `extensions{{extensionName}}.test.tsx`.

