Catalog processor may throw an unbounded error payload to status and exhaust database storage

### 🔎 Search Terms

```plain
catalog
```

### 🗃️ Project Area

Catalog

### 📝 Description & Context

When a catalog processor throws an error that contains a large payload, the catalog copies the whole error into `entity.status.items` without any size limit. This happens with HTTP client errors like Octokit's `RequestError`, which carry the full `response.data`. Because of how the error is wrapped and serialized, the same payload ends up in the stitched entity **several times**:

* `status.items[0].message`
* `status.items[0].error.message`
* `status.items[0].error.cause.message`
* `status.items[0].error.cause.response.data`

Example entity from `final_entities` table:

```json
{
# [...OMITTED...]
"status": {
    "items": [
      {
        "type": "backstage.io/catalog-processing",
        "level": "error",
        "message": "InputError: Processor [REDACTED] threw an error while preprocessing; caused by HttpError: \n\n [... OMITTED ... ]",
        "error": {
          "name": "InputError",
          "message": "Processor [REDACTED] threw an error while preprocessing; caused by HttpError: \n\n[... OMITTED ... ]",
          "cause": {
            "name": "HttpError",
            "status": 503,
            "request": {
              "method": "GET",
              "url": "[REDACTED]",
              "headers": {
                "accept": "application/vnd.github.v3+json",
                "user-agent": "octokit-core.js/7.0.6 [REDACTED]",
                "authorization": "bearer [REDACTED]"
              },
              "request": {}
            },
            "response": {
              "url": "[REDACTED]",
              "status": 503,
              "headers": {
                "connection": "keep-alive",
                "content-length": "[REDACTED]",
                "content-type": "text/html",
                "server": "nginx"
              },
              "data": "\n\n[... OMITTED ... ]"
            },
            "message": "\n\n[... OMITTED ... ]"
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

The HTML response body is copied 4 times in one status item.

In our case, each affected entity grew from ~8 KB to ~2.8 MB. The processor ran for every entity, so the same thing happened to all of them at once. The database storage filled quickly, our PostgreSQL instance became unhealthy, and we had to fix the storage manually. Backstage did not recover on its own. Recovery could be possible when the transient error is resolved but this time we ran out of space before that happened.

The same unbounded data is written to both `refresh_state` and`final_entities` tables, taking even more disk space in the database.

### 👍 Expected Behavior

Processing errors in status should  have a size limit in serialized format, for example by truncating message to a reasonable maximum.
